It always helps to understand the whole picture. It’s not enough to say that a brand is growing 10%. I always[ make] data[ points] relevant to my audience by benchmarking it back to something. Is that 10% accelerated or decelerated from the prior year? Is that 10% higher or lower than the category? Is that 10% better or worse than projected estimates? The easiest way to make data mean something is by making a comparative “than” statement.
Visuals are the KEY to bringing[ data to] life and telling the[ story]. As data analysts, it’s our job to make the numbers meaningful. We’re doing the data a disservice every[ time] we copy and paste a raw data chart from Excel onto a slide. To mitigate this, I find it most helpful to ask myself, which hypothesis[ will] I demonstrate here? (See Tip #1.) Then my follow-up question is, what kind of chart or visualization is going to best represent this data and make the[ point] clear? The simplest rule to follow is to visualize one point per slide. Finally

